{% section-card tone="intro" %}
## What is an ethical AI governance platform?

An **ethical AI governance platform** is the decision and evidence layer around an AI
system. It records what the system is allowed to do, who owns each risk, which evidence
supports a release, who approved it, and what should happen when production behavior
leaves the approved operating envelope.

In plain language, the platform turns principles such as fairness, safety, privacy, and
accountability into controls that can block a deployment, restrict an unsafe feature,
route a decision to a person, or trigger an incident response. A policy document alone
cannot do those jobs.

{% callout variant="info" %}
### Core invariant

Every deployed AI version must be traceable to an intended use, an accountable owner,
the evidence used to accept its known risks, and a tested way to restrict or stop it.
If a model can change without re-evaluation, or a failed control cannot stop release,
the organization has documentation rather than governance.
{% /callout %}

{% concept-grid columns="4" %}
{% concept-card title="System inventory" eyebrow="What exists?" icon="boxes" tone="cyan" %}
Connect each use case to its model, data, prompts, tools, deployment, vendors, owners,
users, and affected people. An unregistered system cannot be governed consistently.
{% /concept-card %}
{% concept-card title="Policy engine" eyebrow="What is allowed?" icon="scale" tone="violet" %}
Translate intended use and impact into a review tier, required controls, approval
authority, exceptions, and expiry conditions.
{% /concept-card %}
{% concept-card title="Evidence record" eyebrow="Why was it approved?" icon="file-check" tone="blue" %}
Bind test reports, data lineage, limitations, security review, sign-offs, and accepted
residual risk to the exact version that was evaluated.
{% /concept-card %}
{% concept-card title="Runtime controls" eyebrow="Can it be contained?" icon="shield-check" tone="green" %}
Monitor real outcomes and provide human review, traffic restriction, rollback, model
disablement, and incident escalation appropriate to the use case.
{% /concept-card %}
{% /concept-grid %}
{% /section-card %}

{% section-card accent="violet" %}
## Classify the use case before choosing controls

Risk belongs to the **socio-technical use case**, not to the model name. The same model
may be low impact when it drafts internal notes and high impact when its output affects
employment, credit, health, education, or access to an essential service.

Start with facts that change the consequence of failure:

- **Intended use:** which decision or workflow receives the output?
- **Affected parties:** who receives the benefit, burden, delay, denial, or surveillance?
- **Decision authority:** does the system assist, recommend, or decide without a human
  checkpoint?
- **Data and context:** are personal, sensitive, confidential, or inferred attributes
  involved?
- **Scale and reversibility:** how many people can be affected, and can a bad outcome be
  corrected before harm occurs?

### Lab 1: route a use case to a governance tier

Choose a scenario, change the system's authority, and put sensitive data in or out of
scope. The lab applies a visible teaching policy, reports every matched rule, and changes
the required control set. It is an internal triage model, not a legal classification.

{% interactive-block id="fundamentals/ethical-ai-governance-platforms-calculator" dataFile="/api/content/fundamentals/ethical-ai-governance-platforms/data/governance-tier-policy.json" /%}

The important outcome is not the tier label. It is the explicit reason that each
control is required and the named authority that may accept residual risk. A real
organization must adapt the policy to its jurisdictions, sector, contracts, and risk
tolerance.
{% /section-card %}

{% section-card accent="cyan" %}
## Run governance as a continuous operating loop

The NIST AI Risk Management Framework organizes work into **Govern, Map, Measure, and
Manage**. Govern is cross-cutting; the other functions repeat as the use case, model,
data, users, and operating environment change. The framework is voluntary and
use-case agnostic, so it is an operating structure rather than a compliance certificate.

{% process-flow %}
{% process-step number="1" title="Set authority and policy" label="Govern" %}
Name accountable owners, risk appetite, review tiers, separation of duties, exception
rules, incident authority, and evidence-retention requirements.
{% /process-step %}
{% process-step number="2" title="Describe the real context" label="Map" %}
Document intended and prohibited uses, affected groups, human workflow, dependencies,
foreseeable misuse, failure consequences, and assumptions that could expire.
{% /process-step %}
{% process-step number="3" title="Test relevant risks" label="Measure" %}
Evaluate validity, subgroup outcomes, security, privacy, robustness, explainability,
human factors, and uncertainty against acceptance criteria defined before testing.
{% /process-step %}
{% process-step number="4" title="Act on the evidence" label="Manage" %}
Mitigate, restrict, accept, transfer, or avoid risk; approve only the evaluated version;
then monitor, respond, and repeat when the system or context changes.
{% /process-step %}
{% /process-flow %}

NIST publishes the [AI RMF Core](https://airc.nist.gov/airmf-resources/airmf/5-sec-core/)
and a voluntary [AI RMF Playbook](https://airc.nist.gov/airmf-resources/playbook/).
The Playbook offers suggested actions, not a checklist that guarantees trustworthiness.
{% /section-card %}

{% section-card accent="blue" %}
## Make the release gate part of the delivery path

A governance decision should travel with the artifact it controls. Store immutable
references to evidence rather than a mutable folder named "approved," and verify them
again when a deployment is promoted.

{% system-flow title="Evidence should control the deployed version" caption="The gate joins policy, test evidence, accountable approval, and the exact artifact. Monitoring sends new evidence back into the same decision loop." %}
{% system-node title="Use-case record" eyebrow="1. Scope" icon="clipboard-list" tone="cyan" %}
Defines intended use, prohibited uses, affected parties, owner, governance tier, and
the assumptions under which the classification remains valid.
{% /system-node %}
{% system-node title="Evidence bundle" eyebrow="2. Prove" icon="file-check" tone="blue" %}
Contains versioned test results, data and model lineage, limitations, review notes,
security findings, and unresolved risks with owners and due dates.
{% /system-node %}
{% system-node title="Policy gate" eyebrow="3. Decide" icon="shield-check" tone="violet" %}
Checks required evidence and approval authority. Missing, stale, or mismatched evidence
blocks promotion instead of creating a warning people can ignore.
{% /system-node %}
{% system-node title="Controlled runtime" eyebrow="4. Observe" icon="activity" tone="green" %}
Emits outcomes by relevant segment, records overrides and complaints, detects drift,
and can restrict or stop the approved version when thresholds are crossed.
{% /system-node %}
{% /system-flow %}

### Lab 2: assemble an auditable release decision

Select a change type and inspect which evidence becomes mandatory. Check off available
artifacts to see whether the release is blocked, conditionally reviewable, or ready for
the named approver. Unlike the first lab, this decision is about **proof for one
version**, not the inherent risk of the use case.

{% interactive-block id="fundamentals/ethical-ai-governance-platforms-release-gate-lab" dataFile="/api/content/fundamentals/ethical-ai-governance-platforms/data/release-evidence-policy.json" /%}

Evidence should expire when its assumptions no longer match reality. A new data source,
new population, changed model behavior, expanded autonomy, new tool permission, or
material prompt change can require targeted re-evaluation even when the model identifier
has not changed.
{% /section-card %}

{% section-card accent="green" %}
## Measure harm in the context of the decision

Fairness is not one universal score. Choose metrics from the failure being controlled,
then inspect both outcome differences and the quality of the underlying estimate.

{% concept-grid columns="3" %}
{% concept-card title="Selection rate" eyebrow="Allocation" icon="users" tone="cyan" %}
Compare how often groups receive a positive outcome. This can reveal allocation gaps,
but equal rates may be inappropriate when the relevant underlying conditions differ.
{% /concept-card %}
{% concept-card title="True-positive rate" eyebrow="Opportunity" icon="target" tone="green" %}
Among people who truly meet the positive condition, compare how often the system finds
them. This is useful when missed positive cases are the central harm.
{% /concept-card %}
{% concept-card title="False-positive rate" eyebrow="Burden" icon="triangle-alert" tone="amber" %}
Among people who do not meet the condition, compare how often the system flags them.
This matters when an incorrect flag creates investigation, denial, or stigma.
{% /concept-card %}
{% /concept-grid %}

For every reported difference, record:

- the population, time window, label definition, and decision threshold;
- group sample sizes and uncertainty, not only a point estimate;
- intersectional slices that the product can responsibly measure;
- likely causes, including data collection, labels, missingness, workflow, and feedback;
- the acceptance criterion, accountable reviewer, mitigation, and retest date.

Do not declare a system "fair" because one ratio crosses a generic threshold. Different
fairness criteria can conflict, labels may encode historical decisions, small samples
can be unstable, and the same model can have different impacts in different workflows.
{% /section-card %}

{% section-card accent="amber" %}
